<KickoffSession
    documentTitle="Scale it here. | Canada2080"
    description="Barry Wylant on three Alberta firms that scaled by selling, and on what manufacturing and AI will take in Canada, from his cleaned remarks at the Canada2080 kickoff."
    eyebrow="Capacity · 1:55 p.m."
    headline="Scale it here."
    name="Barry Wylant"
    bio="Barry Wylant is Associate Professor and Graduate Program Director at SAPL, University of Calgary. An industrial designer and design researcher, he examines how design, technology, and community impact intersect."
    profileUrl="https://www.linkedin.com/in/barry-wylant-105488100/"
    podiumSrc="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5406.jpg"
    podiumAlt="Barry Wylant on the screen, joining the kickoff remotely."
    photos={[
        {
            src: "/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5406.jpg",
            alt: "Barry Wylant on the screen, joining the kickoff remotely.",
            caption: "On the screen.",
        },
    ]}
>
    <p class="lede">
        The Alberta firms he worked with reached the next scale by selling, not
        by scaling themselves.
    </p>
    <p>
        He spoke as an industrial designer, from his own career, and he is
        based in Alberta. Industrial design, as he defined it, is the design of
        products for manufacturing: the chair, the pen, the automobile, the
        cement mixer.
    </p>
    <p>
        Three companies. Each reached the limit of what it could design and
        manufacture locally. Each then sold to a multinational that already had
        design resources, and designed the next generation from there.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Chariot Carriers made child bicycle trailers you can tow behind a
            bike, and built an international presence. He worked on components,
            including the Wing, plastic wheels, and hitches. Thule bought the
            company.
        </li>
        <li>
            Tenet Medical Engineering develops products for orthopaedic
            surgery. Smith &amp; Nephew bought it.
        </li>
        <li>
            Dynastream Innovations made personal activity monitors just outside
            Calgary. Garmin bought it.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        His question: can Canada scale AI, or manufacturing, in a way that
        makes it a player on the world stage?
    </p>
    <p>
        His history of the Auto Pact starts in the early 1960s. Canadian plants
        made standard transmissions. The United States was moving to
        automatics, and that expertise was not here. Canada put a 25 percent
        tariff on cars entering with automatic transmissions. The United States
        disliked the tariff. The two countries negotiated the Auto Pact, which
        became NAFTA, and then its most recent form.
    </p>
    <p>
        Expo 67 is when Canada came of age in design. The Maple Leaf flag was
        two years old. Out of that expo, the International Council of Societies
        of Industrial Design was founded and headquartered in Montreal. It is
        now the World Design Organization. The push through the 1960s and 1970s
        was to lead Canadian industry by design. By the 1990s, he said, China
        had become the world's manufacturer, and the dynamic changed.
    </p>
    <p>
        Canada is a medium-sized nation with a small population, and scaling
        manufacturing is hard. It is a place of innovation. More often than
        not, he said, that innovation is bought by an international firm that
        can take it further.
    </p>
    <p>Three questions he left for the 2080 horizon.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>What does scaling AI in Canada look like over that time?</li>
        <li>Is there a way to use it in many different ways?</li>
        <li>
            What will it take to support it, and is there a Canadian
            perspective on how the industry unfolds?
        </li>
    </ul>
    <Fragment slot="transcript">
        <p class="transcript-note">Cleaned up transcripts.</p>
        <div class="transcript-columns">
            <div>
                <h3>Keynote</h3>
        <blockquote>
            <p>
                Thank you for that kind introduction. Thanks, Francis, for
                inviting me to join you folks today.
            </p>
            <p>
                Today I will be speaking as an industrial designer, sharing
                some ideas from my own perspective on the question of scale.
                This arose within my own career going back a number of years. I
                don't know how familiar people are with industrial design, so
                maybe I'll back up and give a quick introduction. Industrial
                design is the design of products for manufacturing. It includes
                everything from the chairs you're sitting in and the pen you're
                using, all the way through to automobiles, transportation,
                cement mixers, and all manner of artifice that is manufactured.
            </p>
            <p>
                Going back a few years, I worked for three Alberta-based
                companies. I'm based in Alberta myself.
            </p>
            <p>
                Chariot Carriers. They designed child bicycle trailers that you
                can tow behind your bike. They managed to create an
                international presence with their product. We designed a number
                of components that were part of their overall product line,
                including the Wing on the side, plastic wheels, and hitches.
            </p>
            <p>
                Tenet Medical Engineering. They develop products for
                orthopaedic surgery.
            </p>
            <p>
                Dynastream Innovations. They were doing personal activity
                monitors based just outside of Calgary.
            </p>
            <p>
                Each and every one of these companies reached a certain point
                in their scaling based on what they could design and
                manufacture locally. Correlation doesn't equal causation, but
                it is an interesting observation that they were all able to
                leverage the work they had done to get to next-scale
                production. However, they did not do this by scaling
                themselves, but by selling to multinationals.
            </p>
            <p>Chariot was bought by Thule.</p>
            <p>Tenet was bought by Smith &amp; Nephew.</p>
            <p>Dynastream was bought by Garmin.</p>
            <p>
                Once acquired by those larger entities, which already had
                design resources, they were able to design for the next
                generation of products by accessing those international-scale
                resources.
            </p>
            <p>
                For me, this raises the question of scale. Is Canada able to
                scale in an appropriate way with regards to AI, or almost any
                aspect of manufacturing, and understand what it takes to be a
                player on the world stage?
            </p>
            <p>
                It's interesting if you look into the history of Canada. Going
                back to the 1960s, the reason we had free trade was because of
                automatic transmissions for cars. In the early 1960s, Canada's
                auto manufacturers basically manufactured exclusively standard
                transmissions. The US was moving ahead on automatic
                transmissions, and that expertise did not exist in Canada. In
                fact, Canada had a 25% tariff on US cars coming into Canada
                with automatic transmissions.
            </p>
            <p>
                It's an interesting historical precedent. The US didn't like
                the tariff situation, so Canada and the US negotiated the Auto
                Pact. The Auto Pact eventually evolved into NAFTA, and into its
                most recent iteration.
            </p>
            <p>
                The other big thing that happened in the 1960s was Expo 67.
                What's really interesting about that is Canada was seen
                internationally as coming of age in a design sense. The Maple
                Leaf flag was two years old at that point, and Expo was a way
                to showcase Canada as a world leader in design. Coming out of
                that, the International Council of Societies of Industrial
                Design was born and headquartered in Montreal, now known as the
                World Design Organization.
            </p>
            <p>
                There was a big push coming out of the '60s and '70s to advance
                Canadian design as a way of leading Canadian industry and
                scaling. How do we make Canada a fantastic manufacturer? What
                ended up happening, though, was China became the world's
                manufacturer in the 1990s, leading to a very different dynamic.
            </p>
            <p>
                When we look at the historical roots of how manufacturing has
                evolved within Canada, we recognize that as a medium-sized
                nation with a small population, it is actually very difficult
                to scale the manufacturing atmosphere. Canada is a bastion of
                innovation, but more often than not, that innovation is
                acquired by international entities that can scale it to the
                next level.
            </p>
            <p>
                Given the conversation around AI and understanding the future
                thresholds for Canada 2080, there is a very compelling
                investigation required. What does the scaling of AI look like
                in Canada over that time horizon? Is there a way to leverage it
                in many different ways? What is it going to take to support it,
                and is there a uniquely Canadian perspective on how this
                industry unfolds over the coming decades?
            </p>
            <p>Thank you very much.</p>
        </blockquote>
            </div>
